## TL;DR  
On Android the **MapLibre-Native** SDK does not have a `addProtocol` helper like the web version.  
Instead you give the SDK a custom **`HttpRequestHandler`** (or an OkHttp interceptor) that is called for **every URL** the style asks for – custom schemes such as `vault://…` are treated the same way as `https://…`.  

Implement the handler, register it **once** (before you create the `MapView`), and return a `HttpResponse` that contains the decrypted tile bytes.

Below is a complete, ready-to-copy Kotlin example that shows:

1. How to parse the `vault://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}` URL.  
2. How to decrypt the blob (replace the stub with your own crypto).  
3. How to register the handler with MapLibre.  
4. How to use the URL in a style JSON / `VectorSource`.

---

## 1. The interface you need to implement

```kotlin
import org.maplibre.android.http.HttpRequest
import org.maplibre.android.http.HttpResponse
import org.maplibre.android.http.HttpRequestHandler

class VaultRequestHandler(
    private val vault: TileVault   // your own class that knows how to read & decrypt
) : HttpRequestHandler {

    override fun handleRequest(request: HttpRequest): HttpResponse {
        val url = request.url

        // Only handle our custom scheme – everything else we let the default
        // network stack deal with (by returning a 404 which makes the SDK fall back
        // to its internal OkHttp client).
        if (url.scheme != "vault") {
            return HttpResponse(404, null, 0L)
        }

        // Expected pattern: vault://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}
        // url.path = "/tiles/12/3456/7890"
        val segments = url.path.trimStart('/').split('/')
        if (segments.size != 4 || segments[0] != "tiles") {
            return HttpResponse(400, null, 0L)   // bad request
        }

        val z = segments[1].toIntOrNull()
        val x = segments[2].toIntOrNull()
        val y = segments[3].toIntOrNull()
        if (z == null || x == null || y == null) {
            return HttpResponse(400, null, 0L)
        }

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 1️⃣  Read the encrypted blob from wherever you store it
        // 2️⃣  Decrypt it
        // 3️⃣  Return the raw MVT bytes
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        return try {
            val encrypted = vault.readEncryptedTile(z, x, y)   // ByteArray
            val plain = vault.decrypt(encrypted)              // ByteArray

            // MapLibre expects an InputStream and the length of the body.
            HttpResponse(
                code = 200,
                body = plain.inputStream(),
                contentLength = plain.size.toLong()
            )
        } catch (e: Exception) {
            // Anything that goes wrong should be reported as a 404/500 so the SDK
            // can fall back to the “no-tile” placeholder.
            HttpResponse(404, null, 0L)
        }
    }
}
```

### What `HttpResponse` means

| Field | Meaning |
|-------|---------|
| `code` | HTTP-like status (200 = OK, 404 = not found, 400 = bad request, …). |
| `body` | An `InputStream` that will be read **once** by the SDK. |
| `contentLength` | Size of the stream (or `-1` if unknown). |

> **Important** – The handler runs on a background thread managed by the SDK, **not** on the UI thread. Do **not** touch UI components here.

---

## 2. Register the handler **once** (usually in `Application.onCreate`)

```kotlin
import android.app.Application
import org.maplibre.android.MapLibre
import org.maplibre.android.MapLibreOptions

class MyApp : Application() {

    override fun onCreate() {
        super.onCreate()

        // 1️⃣  Create your own vault implementation (could be a Room DB,
        //     encrypted file, etc.).
        val vault = TileVault(this)   // <-- your own class

        // 2️⃣  Build the MapLibre options and plug the handler in.
        val options = MapLibreOptions()
            .httpRequestHandler(VaultRequestHandler(vault))

        // 3️⃣  Initialise the SDK *before* any MapView is inflated.
        MapLibre.getInstance(this, options)
    }
}
```

> If you already called `MapLibre.getInstance(context)` elsewhere, you can also set the handler later with  
> `MapLibre.getInstance(context).setHttpRequestHandler(VaultRequestHandler(vault))`  
> but doing it at start-up guarantees the handler is used for **all** map instances.

