## 4. Alternative: OkHttp interceptor (if you already use OkHttp)

MapLibre-Native uses **OkHttp** under the hood. If you prefer to stay in the OkHttp world you can supply a custom `OkHttpClient` that contains an interceptor for the `vault://` scheme.

```kotlin
val interceptor = Interceptor { chain ->
    val request = chain.request()
    val url = request.url

    if (url.scheme == "vault") {
        // Same parsing/decryption logic as above …
        val bytes = vault.decrypt(vault.readEncryptedTile(...))
        val responseBody = bytes.toResponseBody(null)   // no media type needed
        return@Interceptor Response.Builder()
            .request(request)
            .protocol(Protocol.HTTP_1_1)
            .code(200)
            .message("OK")
            .body(responseBody)
            .build()
    }

    // Let the default client handle everything else
    chain.proceed(request)
}

val client = OkHttpClient.Builder()
    .addInterceptor(interceptor)
    .build()

val options = MapLibreOptions()
    .okHttpClient(client)          // <-- inject the client
MapLibre.getInstance(this, options)
```

Both approaches (the `HttpRequestHandler` API or a custom `OkHttpClient`) achieve the same result; the former is the **official, SDK-agnostic** way and works even if MapLibre switches its networking stack in a future version.

---

## 5. Gotchas & best-practice notes

| Issue | What to watch for |
|-------|-------------------|
| **Threading** | The handler runs on a thread from the SDK’s internal pool. Do **not** block the UI. Heavy decryption should be fine here, but avoid long-running I/O that could starve other requests. |
| **Caching** | MapLibre already caches tile responses (by URL) in its internal `TileStore`. Because the URL is deterministic (`vault://tiles/…`), the decrypted bytes will be cached automatically. If you want a custom cache policy, you can return appropriate HTTP headers (`Cache-Control`) in the `HttpResponse`. |
| **Error handling** | Return a non-200 status (e.g. 404) if the tile cannot be read or decrypted. The SDK will render the “no-tile” placeholder and continue loading other tiles. |
| **Multiple sources** | If you have more than one custom scheme (e.g. `vault://raster/{z}/{x}/{y}`) you can keep a single handler and branch on `url.host` or `url.path`. |
| **Testing** | Use `MapLibre.getInstance(context).httpRequestHandler` to swap in a mock handler during UI tests. |
| **ProGuard / R8** | If you use reflection-based crypto libraries, make sure they are kept (`-keep class ...`). The handler itself does not need any special rules. |

