Below is a complete, ready-to-copy Kotlin recipe that does the same thing on Android that `maplibregl.addProtocol()` does on the web.

---

## 1. What MapLibre Android calls a “protocol handler”

In the native SDK every URL that a source (or a sprite, glyphs, …) asks for is routed through an **`HttpRequestHandler`**.  
If you supply your own implementation, you can:

* recognise any scheme you like (`vault://…` in your case)  
* read the encrypted blob from wherever you store it (file, SQLCipher DB, Keystore, …)  
* decrypt it on the fly  
* hand the raw bytes back to the renderer as an `InputStream`

That is the exact analogue of `maplibregl.addProtocol()`.

---

## 2. Minimal dependencies

```gradle
dependencies {
    implementation "org.maplibre.gl:android-sdk:10.5.0"   // or the latest version
}
```

> **NOTE** – The SDK is a pure native library, no Mapbox token is required.

---

## 3. Implement the handler

```kotlin
package com.example.maplibrevault

import android.net.Uri
import org.maplibre.android.http.HttpRequest
import org.maplibre.android.http.HttpResponse
import org.maplibre.android.http.HttpRequestHandler
import java.io.ByteArrayInputStream
import java.io.InputStream

/**
 * Handles every request that the MapLibre engine makes.
 * If the URL starts with the custom scheme "vault://", we decrypt the tile
 * and return it.  All other URLs are delegated to the default network handler.
 */
class VaultRequestHandler(
    private val defaultHandler: HttpRequestHandler = HttpRequestHandler.DEFAULT
) : HttpRequestHandler {

    override fun handleRequest(request: HttpRequest): HttpResponse {
        val uri = Uri.parse(request.url)

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 1️⃣  Is this a request we want to handle ourselves?
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        if (uri.scheme == "vault") {
            // Expected pattern: vault://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}
            // Example: vault://tiles/12/657/1583
            val pathSegments = uri.pathSegments
            if (pathSegments.size >= 4 && pathSegments[0] == "tiles") {
                val z = pathSegments[1].toInt()
                val x = pathSegments[2].toInt()
                val y = pathSegments[3].toInt()

                // -----------------------------------------------------------------
                // 2️⃣  Load the encrypted blob from your storage and decrypt it.
                // -----------------------------------------------------------------
                val decryptedBytes: ByteArray = decryptTile(z, x, y)

                // -----------------------------------------------------------------
                // 3️⃣  Build the response that MapLibre expects.
                // -----------------------------------------------------------------
                return HttpResponse.Builder()
                    .setStatusCode(200)
                    .setBody(ByteArrayInputStream(decryptedBytes))
                    .addHeader("Content-Type", "application/x-protobuf") // MVT mime type
                    .build()
            }

            // If the URL looks like vault:// but we cannot parse it, return 400.
            return HttpResponse.Builder()
                .setStatusCode(400)
                .setBody(null)
                .build()
        }

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 4️⃣  Anything else (http, https, file, …) falls back to the default
        //     network handler so you keep normal behaviour for sprites,
        //     glyphs, remote vector sources, etc.
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        return defaultHandler.handleRequest(request)
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Your own decryption routine – replace with whatever you actually use.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    private fun decryptTile(z: Int, x: Int, y: Int): ByteArray {
        // Example: tiles are stored under app-private files dir as
        //   /data/user/0/com.example.app/files/vault/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.mvt.enc
        val encryptedFile = File(
            context.filesDir,
            "vault/tiles/$z/$x/$y.mvt.enc"
        )

        // Read the encrypted bytes
        val encrypted = encryptedFile.readBytes()

        // Decrypt – this is just a placeholder.  Use your real crypto code.
        return MyCrypto.decrypt(encrypted)
    }
}
```

### What you need to replace

| Piece | What to replace |
|------|-----------------|
| `decryptTile()` | Your actual decryption logic (AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305, etc.) |
| `MyCrypto.decrypt()` | The class/method that turns the encrypted blob into the raw MVT bytes |
| `context` | Pass an Android `Context` (e.g. via constructor) if you need it to locate files |

