## 6. Full-working example (single file)

```kotlin
// MainActivity.kt
package com.example.maplibrevault

import android.os.Bundle
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity
import org.maplibre.android.maps.MapView
import org.maplibre.android.maps.Style

class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {

    private lateinit var mapView: MapView

    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)

        // Layout contains just a <com.mapbox.mapboxsdk.maps.MapView .../>
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)
        mapView = findViewById(R.id.mapView)

        mapView.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
        mapView.getMapAsync { mapboxMap ->
            mapboxMap.setStyle(
                Style.Builder()
                    .fromUri("asset://my_style.json")   // <-- references vault://tiles/…
            )
        }
    }

    // ---- forward lifecycle events to the MapView ----
    override fun onStart() = super.onStart().also { mapView.onStart() }
    override fun onResume() = super.onResume().also { mapView.onResume() }
    override fun onPause() = super.onPause().also { mapView.onPause() }
    override fun onStop() = super.onStop().also { mapView.onStop() }
    override fun onLowMemory() = super.onLowMemory().also { mapView.onLowMemory() }
    override fun onDestroy() = super.onDestroy().also { mapView.onDestroy() }
    override fun onSaveInstanceState(outState: Bundle) {
        super.onSaveInstanceState(outState)
        mapView.onSaveInstanceState(outState)
    }
}
```

All the heavy lifting (decrypt-on-the-fly) lives in `VaultRequestHandler`. The rest of the app is unchanged.

---

## 7. Things to keep in mind

| Topic | Detail |
|-------|--------|
| **Threading** | `handleRequest()` is called on a background thread by the native renderer, so you can do I/O and decryption here without blocking the UI. |
| **Caching** | If you want to cache the decrypted tiles, you can either write them to a temporary file and return a `file://` URL, or you can keep them in memory and return the same `byte[]` for repeated requests. The SDK does its own HTTP-cache for `http/https` URLs, but it does **not** cache custom schemes automatically. |
| **Error handling** | Return a `404` or `500` response (via `HttpResponse.Builder().setStatusCode(..)`) if the tile cannot be found or decryption fails. The map will simply show a missing-tile placeholder. |
| **Other resources** | The same handler also receives requests for sprites (`vault://sprites/...`), glyphs, and even raster tiles. You can branch on `uri.pathSegments[0]` to treat them differently. |
| **Testing** | You can unit-test the handler by constructing a fake `HttpRequest` and asserting that the returned `HttpResponse.body` contains the expected bytes. |
| **Performance tip** | Decrypt only the exact tile requested; avoid loading an entire zoom-level into memory. Use a fast symmetric cipher (AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305) and keep the key in the Android Keystore. |

